  annotation "note_1771575909050_2", anchor: "chipping away the ☽'ofund' Guild's sealed-chamber warning glyphs for profit", text: "Those glyphs are not decorative. They are pressure-scored hazard encodings: corruption saturation levels, breach proximity, the characteristic frequency of something moving beneath the vault floor. The Exchange's engineers would have recognized pressure-scoring — it is taught in the first season of structural apprenticeship. They chipped through it because the vaults behind contained unprocessed fire-cores worth more than the engineers' combined salaries for a decade. The corruption that later bloomed in Wane☽ Oncea's lower levels originated from those unsealed chambers. The ice-memory progression is sequential and clear. Someone approved the removal order. That order has not surfaced.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771575909050_3", anchor: "at least two seasons prior", text: "Conservative, and I knew it when I wrote it. Courier logs in the Foundation Depths show weight discrepancies flagged at the Exchange's council tier four seasons before Nyla's confession — not two. The earlier filings were re-dated during what the Exchange called a \"ledger reconciliation.\" I found the originals in a stratum of administrative ice that someone attempted to melt and refreeze. Attempted. The ice remembers the attempt too.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771575909050_4", anchor: "The Wake-Singers, an orca cult operating from the Corpse Current, eventually recovered Wane☽ Oncea", text: "\"Recovered\" is doing diplomatic work. The relationship registry lists the Wake-Singers as Exchange allies. This entry describes them taking territory. Both cannot be simultaneously true unless \"alliance\" now means \"they stopped killing us after we returned what we stole.\" Which — given the Flipper Accord's own logic — perhaps it does.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
